 ## Subtopics
 
 ### [Capture-Conditioned Learning Allowance](10214-capture-conditioned-learning-allowance.md)
-Determines the target Gaussian population size based on capture characteristics, specifically using track-adjusted pixels normalized against standard scenes, to replace fixed defaults. It sets apart from sibling methods by ensuring the model budget is invariant to redundant views (e.g., duplicated images), whereas alternatives like total pixel count scale with view count and SfM point counts scale with matched-observation density rather than capture scale.
+A method to determine the target Gaussian population size for 3D Gaussian Splatting based on capture characteristics, specifically using track-adjusted pixels normalized against standard scenes, to replace fixed defaults. It sets apart from sibling methods by ensuring the model budget is invariant to redundant views (e.g., duplicated images), whereas alternatives like total pixel count scale with view count and SfM point counts scale with matched-observation density rather than capture scale.
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** The allowance calculation is $O(1)$ with respect to scene complexity, depending only on $N_{\text{img}}$. Sensitivity scoring requires a backward pass per view, similar to standard 3DGS training, but avoids additional scoring renders [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Memory usage scales with the final Gaussian count $N_G$. TangoGS typically uses 48% fewer Gaussians than the best-performing baseline (LeGS) on standard scenes while matching PSNR [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Performance:** On 13 standard benchmark scenes, TangoGS matches the mean PSNR of LeGS with significantly fewer Gaussians [S1, unofficial]. On 8 large captures, it automatically scales to larger models without retuning [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Strengths:** Single configuration for all 21 tested captures; invariant to redundant views; automatic scaling; no per-capture tuning required [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Limitations:** Requires accurate track-adjusted pixel counts; relies on PSNR as a proxy for quality; guard aborts prevent catastrophic collapse but may leave suboptimal populations if pruning thresholds are misaligned [S1, unofficial].
-*   **When to use:** When deploying 3DGS across diverse capture sizes (e.g., from small indoor scenes to large outdoor environments) without manual hyperparameter adjustment [S1, unofficial].
-*   **When not to use:** If per-capture optimization is acceptable and maximum possible PSNR is required regardless of Gaussian count, standard tuned 3DGS or LeGS might be preferred [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Time Complexity:** Training time scales with the number of Gaussians and iterations. TangoGS uses a shared configuration across all 21 captures, whereas baselines require per-capture tuning [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** Memory usage is proportional to the final Gaussian count $N_G$. TangoGS matches the mean PSNR of the best-performing evaluated baseline, LeGS, with 48% fewer Gaussians on 13 standard benchmark scenes [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Performance:** On 13 standard benchmark scenes, TangoGS matches the mean PSNR of LeGS with 48% fewer Gaussians [S1, unofficial]. On 8 large captures, the same configuration automatically scales to larger models when necessary [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Strengths:** Automatic scaling across different capture sizes without retuning; robustness to redundant views; reduced model size compared to baselines.
+*   **Limitations:** Requires accurate capture metadata (image count, track data); sensitivity scoring adds overhead to the backward pass (though LiteGS implements it efficiently without additional renders) [S1, unofficial].
+*   **When to Use:** When deploying 3D Gaussian Splatting across diverse capture sizes without manual hyperparameter tuning.
+*   **When Not to Use:** When per-capture optimization is feasible and maximum possible PSNR is required regardless of model size.
 
 ## Background
-TangoGS was introduced in the paper "Gauss What You Need: Compact Gaussian Splatting Across Scene Scales" [S1, unofficial]. It builds on the principles of 3D Gaussian Splatting densification strategies, specifically incorporating sensitivity scoring from LiteGS/Speedy-Splat [S1, unofficial]. The method addresses the limitation of fixed Gaussian counts in standard 3DGS, which do not scale well across different capture sizes [S1, unofficial].
+TangoGS was introduced in the paper "Gauss What You Need: Compact Gaussian Splatting Across Scene Scales" [S1, unofficial]. It builds on the 3D Gaussian Splatting framework and integrates concepts from LiteGS for sensitivity scoring [S1, unofficial]. The method addresses the challenge of scaling Gaussian Splatting to different capture sizes without manual intervention, a common issue in inverse volume rendering.
 
